Vulnerability Title
Client-Side OTP Resend Cooldown Bypass via Response Manipulation

OWASP Category
A04:2021 – Insecure Design (Improper enforcement of business logic and rate-limiting intervals)

CVSS 3.1 Vector
CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:L/A:N

Description
The application implements a 60-second cooldown timer before a user can request a new Email One-Time Passcode (OTP) during the password reset flow. However, this cooldown duration is controlled client-side via the "cool_down_in_seconds" parameter returned in the server's HTTP response from the /api/ido/api/v2/auth/assert endpoint.   
1.jpg
+ 2

Because the server does not enforce the 60-second interval on the backend, an attacker can intercept and modify the response value from 60 to a lower value (such as 6). This allows triggering multiple OTP emails within seconds instead of waiting for the intended cooldown period. While the backend enforces a hard cap of 5 OTPs per session, the per-request cooldown restriction is completely bypassed.   
2.jpg
+ 3

Impact
Bypass of Security Controls: Attackers can defeat the intended 60-second throttling mechanism, allowing rapid generation of up to 5 OTP emails within seconds.   
3.jpg
+ 1

User Annoyance & Rapid Account Lockout: An attacker knowing a victim's email address can rapidly exhaust the user's 5-OTP limit in a few seconds, potentially causing minor email spam and temporarily locking the legitimate user out of the password reset flow.

Increased Resource Consumption: Rapidly triggering email delivery services unnecessarily increases SMTP/gateway load, even within the 5-OTP cap.

Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps & Captions
Step 1: Navigate to the Morgan Stanley Matrix UAT login page ([https://auth.matrix-uat.ms.com/login](https://auth.matrix-uat.ms.com/login)), enter a valid username, and click on the Reset/Forgot Password link.

PoC 1 Caption: Initiating the password reset flow by clicking the "Reset/Forgot Password" link on the login page.


1.jpg
Step 2: Intercept the response to the POST /api/ido/api/v2/auth/assert request using an intercepting proxy (e.g., Burp Suite) and observe the "cool_down_in_seconds":60 parameter under the "resend" action in the JSON body.

PoC 2 Caption: Intercepted HTTP response from /api/ido/api/v2/auth/assert showing the default "cool_down_in_seconds":60 parameter.


2.jpg
Step 3: Modify the "cool_down_in_seconds" value in the intercepted response from 60 to 6 and forward the response to the browser.

PoC 3 Caption: Manipulating the "cool_down_in_seconds" parameter from 60 to 6 in the HTTP response.


3.jpg
Step 4: Observe the "Verify with Email OTP" page in the browser; the resend countdown timer now starts from 6 seconds instead of 60 seconds, allowing the Resend option to be triggered immediately after 6 seconds.

PoC 4 Caption: Application UI reflecting the manipulated cooldown timer ("Resend code in 1s") instead of the standard 60-second wait.


4.jpg
Step 5: Click the resend option after 6 seconds and check the registered email inbox to confirm multiple distinct OTP emails are delivered within the same minute (up to the maximum limit of 5 OTPs).

PoC 5 Caption: Email inbox showing two separate OTPs (25614290 and 10402778) received simultaneously at 11:25 AM, confirming the 60-second cooldown bypass.


5.png
Mitigation
Enforce Cooldown Server-Side: Track the timestamp of the last OTP generated for each user/session on the backend. If a resend request arrives before 60 seconds have elapsed since the previous OTP was issued, reject the request with an HTTP 429 Too Many Requests status code, regardless of client-side timer states.

Treat Client-Side Timers as UI-Only: Use the "cool_down_in_seconds" response field strictly for user interface display purposes, never as a security boundary.

Maintain Session & Account Caps: Retain the existing maximum limit of 5 OTPs per session/window alongside strict server-side interval checks.

